WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.925 --> 00:00:04.134
- Anytime I deviated from that love and

00:00:04.134 --> 00:00:07.147
went into something else
I just was so unhappy.

00:00:07.147 --> 00:00:09.802
But even within the
careers that we're pursuing

00:00:09.802 --> 00:00:12.920
we can train ourselves
to think like outsiders

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.373
and help loosen up the
mind in the process.

00:00:15.373 --> 00:00:18.320
I once counted with a
girlfriend that I had probably

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.652
over 80 jobs.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:23.041
You have this like wealth of information

00:00:23.041 --> 00:00:25.435
and knowledge and
ability to acquire skills

00:00:25.435 --> 00:00:27.375
that nobody a hundred years ago,

00:00:27.375 --> 00:00:29.224
20 years ago could dream about.

00:00:29.224 --> 00:00:33.391
Often what separates creative
from conventional thinkers

00:00:34.881 --> 00:00:38.135
is that they simply search wider.

00:00:38.135 --> 00:00:39.744
Self-reliance is to me

00:00:39.744 --> 00:00:42.101
the most, if you're going
to develop anything in life

00:00:42.101 --> 00:00:43.689
that that has to be it

00:00:43.689 --> 00:00:45.092
that's the most important skill.

00:00:45.092 --> 00:00:47.223
You need to be able to
put up with fools and

00:00:47.223 --> 00:00:49.369
laugh at them and not take them seriously.

00:00:49.369 --> 00:00:51.351
Life is inherently chaotic and it

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:53.632
doesn't fit into our tidy formulas.

00:00:53.632 --> 00:00:55.715
Self-transformation we think comes

00:00:55.715 --> 00:00:57.811
through a spiritual journey.

00:00:57.811 --> 00:01:01.029
Therapy, a guru who tells us what to do.

00:01:01.029 --> 00:01:02.748
When you are motivated,

00:01:02.748 --> 00:01:05.144
when you feel yourself emotionally engaged

00:01:05.144 --> 00:01:08.001
in the subject you learn faster.

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:09.730
- He's an American author and speaker

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:11.753
known for his books on strategy

00:01:11.753 --> 00:01:13.138
power and seduction.

00:01:13.138 --> 00:01:15.778
He has written five
international bestsellers.

00:01:15.778 --> 00:01:17.832
His work has been featured
in the New York Times

00:01:17.832 --> 00:01:20.444
the New Yorker, CNN, USA Today

00:01:20.444 --> 00:01:22.384
and many more media publications.

00:01:22.384 --> 00:01:24.139
He's Robert Greene and here are his

00:01:24.139 --> 00:01:26.306
top ten rules for success.

00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:31.889
The difference between people
who are successful and not

00:01:31.889 --> 00:01:34.539
although those who are
successful seem to know from

00:01:34.539 --> 00:01:37.587
the age of seven or eight, maybe older.

00:01:37.587 --> 00:01:41.274
You know they're very in
tune with what they love.

00:01:41.274 --> 00:01:44.586
They, I compare it to a
voice inside their head.

00:01:44.586 --> 00:01:46.049
Not literally a voice

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:48.710
but something saying you really are

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:50.467
drawn to this subject.

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:52.142
And they hear it throughout their lives.

00:01:52.142 --> 00:01:56.270
And for me, it was it
was writing and books.

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:58.412
Since I was a kid.

00:01:58.412 --> 00:02:01.865
And you know at anytime I deviated

00:02:01.865 --> 00:02:04.828
from that love and went
into something else

00:02:04.828 --> 00:02:06.921
I just was so unhappy.

00:02:06.921 --> 00:02:09.174
I knew that I wasn't
doing the right thing.

00:02:09.174 --> 00:02:11.224
And so it's just like this
voice that keeps drawing

00:02:11.224 --> 00:02:14.304
you back to what you really really love.

00:02:14.304 --> 00:02:16.767
Some of the most important discoveries,

00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:19.033
inventions and innovations

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:22.328
cut within a field, come
from people who are trained

00:02:22.328 --> 00:02:24.314
in a much different field.

00:02:24.314 --> 00:02:27.661
And who have an outsider's approach.

00:02:27.661 --> 00:02:30.576
Louis Pasteur was a
classic example of this.

00:02:30.576 --> 00:02:33.603
He was trained as a
chemist in mineralogist

00:02:33.603 --> 00:02:37.481
so he brought amidst a much
different way of thinking

00:02:37.481 --> 00:02:39.885
to the subject of medicine and germs.

00:02:39.885 --> 00:02:43.468
A method that was
infinitely more creative.

00:02:44.704 --> 00:02:47.138
Albert Einstein was a complete outsider.

00:02:47.138 --> 00:02:49.231
His two most important discoveries came

00:02:49.231 --> 00:02:52.520
when he was working at
the Swiss Patent Office.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.205
Google itself exemplifies this idea.

00:02:56.205 --> 00:02:58.650
It is perhaps the most successful business

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.172
on the planet founded by two men who are

00:03:01.172 --> 00:03:04.273
relatively complete outsiders to business

00:03:04.273 --> 00:03:05.680
and who therefore structured

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.638
and directed their company in a very

00:03:07.638 --> 00:03:09.431
novel and creative fashion.

00:03:09.431 --> 00:03:11.313
I could go on and on and on with this list

00:03:11.313 --> 00:03:13.730
including many great artists.

00:03:14.869 --> 00:03:18.126
There are two important
reasons why outsiders

00:03:18.126 --> 00:03:21.905
generally have a creative advantage.

00:03:21.905 --> 00:03:25.833
First, they are less
steeped in and burdened

00:03:25.833 --> 00:03:27.450
by the conventions of the field

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.119
that they are attacking.

00:03:29.119 --> 00:03:32.341
They ask a different set of questions.

00:03:32.341 --> 00:03:36.508
They approach problems
from unconventional angles.

00:03:37.496 --> 00:03:40.074
And second, they are
often trained in totally

00:03:40.074 --> 00:03:42.112
unrelated fields, and so can make

00:03:42.112 --> 00:03:44.989
interesting and creative connections

00:03:44.989 --> 00:03:48.233
between two very different
forms of thinking.

00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:50.236
Like science and business

00:03:50.236 --> 00:03:51.785
or chemistry and medicine

00:03:51.785 --> 00:03:53.952
or engineering and design.

00:03:55.271 --> 00:03:57.403
Now we can't all consciously

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.720
follow such a path.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.555
But even within the
careers that we're pursuing

00:04:02.555 --> 00:04:05.842
we can train ourselves
to think like outsiders

00:04:05.842 --> 00:04:08.714
and help loosen up the
mind in the process.

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:10.954
I once counted with a
girlfriend that I had

00:04:10.954 --> 00:04:14.226
probably over 80 jobs. (laughs)

00:04:14.226 --> 00:04:17.989
You know I like was a
guide for Greek ruins.

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:20.989
When I worked construction in Crete.

00:04:22.803 --> 00:04:25.720
I taught English here and in Spain.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.788
I did the first like encyclopedia for

00:04:30.788 --> 00:04:34.679
the Internet, I helped
write an encyclopedia.

00:04:34.679 --> 00:04:36.762
I was a busboy, a waiter.

00:04:37.904 --> 00:04:39.935
God I'm not leaving out something juicy.

00:04:39.935 --> 00:04:43.682
Oh, I was a skip tracer I
worked for a detective agency

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:46.733
helping find people who left town you know

00:04:46.733 --> 00:04:49.049
without telling (mumbling)
where they were.

00:04:49.049 --> 00:04:50.216
- Yeah.
- You know.

00:04:50.216 --> 00:04:51.818
- With all those things if
you think about it helps

00:04:51.818 --> 00:04:54.119
because it's like doing
research for a book.

00:04:54.119 --> 00:04:55.237
Or?
- Well the thing

00:04:55.237 --> 00:04:57.337
about being a writer is that

00:04:57.337 --> 00:04:59.773
you have interesting people.
- Yeah.

00:04:59.773 --> 00:05:00.606
- In life.

00:05:00.606 --> 00:05:02.166
And so I'm always wanting
to know what it's like

00:05:02.166 --> 00:05:04.541
you know what it's like to be Shira.

00:05:04.541 --> 00:05:05.940
You know.
- Would you want to know?

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.039
- Yeah I really would.
(laughing)

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:09.866
Because then if I really
knew what made Shira tick

00:05:09.866 --> 00:05:13.912
then Shira would become a
character in my next book.

00:05:13.912 --> 00:05:15.254
- Mmm.
- So

00:05:15.254 --> 00:05:17.054
you got to be curious about people.

00:05:17.054 --> 00:05:18.174
- You do.
- And having

00:05:18.174 --> 00:05:19.790
a lot of different jobs helped out, yeah.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:21.844
There's differences and then there's

00:05:21.844 --> 00:05:23.532
there's not differences.

00:05:23.532 --> 00:05:26.365
So you know the brain is the same.

00:05:27.343 --> 00:05:32.066
The brain evolved over the
course of millions of years.

00:05:32.066 --> 00:05:35.918
So I try to make the point that you know

00:05:35.918 --> 00:05:39.294
what da Vinci was going
through the process is

00:05:39.294 --> 00:05:42.194
that there's a template
there and it's the same.

00:05:42.194 --> 00:05:43.810
Because our brains haven't

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:46.710
really changed in an evolutionary sense

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.332
in four or 500 years.

00:05:48.332 --> 00:05:50.795
On the other hand, the
circumstances are different

00:05:50.795 --> 00:05:52.777
and people now live in a world

00:05:52.777 --> 00:05:55.046
where there's many more distractions

00:05:55.046 --> 00:05:57.471
so it's harder to do that kind of focus

00:05:57.471 --> 00:05:59.139
that I was mentioning.

00:05:59.139 --> 00:06:01.714
So in some ways it's a little bit harder.

00:06:01.714 --> 00:06:04.978
And the people that I've
interviewed are really good

00:06:04.978 --> 00:06:09.571
at shutting out all of the
distractions of the world.

00:06:09.571 --> 00:06:10.840
That's sort of what separates them

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:12.145
every single one of them.

00:06:12.145 --> 00:06:14.524
Paul Graham, you know Paul Graham?

00:06:14.524 --> 00:06:17.156
Paul doesn't have a smartphone.

00:06:17.156 --> 00:06:18.288
You know.

00:06:18.288 --> 00:06:21.194
He doesn't spend time on the Internet.

00:06:21.194 --> 00:06:23.402
He's extremely focused.

00:06:23.402 --> 00:06:25.643
The woman Yōsuke Matsuoka,

00:06:25.643 --> 00:06:29.464
she she does all of her
writing with a pen and paper.

00:06:29.464 --> 00:06:33.242
She's not sitting there day
in and day out on the computer

00:06:33.242 --> 00:06:35.959
checking her Facebook or
Twitter page or whatever.

00:06:35.959 --> 00:06:38.735
I could go through all of them that way.

00:06:38.735 --> 00:06:40.941
Steve Jobs is sort of the ultimate

00:06:40.941 --> 00:06:43.769
icon of mastery of our era.

00:06:43.769 --> 00:06:46.920
And he said the only thing
that's differentiated me

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.805
from everyone else was my level of focus.

00:06:49.805 --> 00:06:52.399
I could take a problem
and I could focus on it

00:06:52.399 --> 00:06:53.560
and burrow into it

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.393
and everybody else was so distracted.

00:06:55.393 --> 00:06:57.441
He knew how to close
his door get away from

00:06:57.441 --> 00:06:59.187
his wife, his kids, everyone

00:06:59.187 --> 00:07:00.322
and just focus.

00:07:00.322 --> 00:07:02.169
So the all of these people

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:06.433
have to overcome the intense
distractions in our world.

00:07:06.433 --> 00:07:08.106
They and they're able to do it.

00:07:08.106 --> 00:07:10.668
On the other hand, what you can get with

00:07:10.668 --> 00:07:14.456
a click on the Internet,
with a Google search

00:07:14.456 --> 00:07:16.588
compared to what da Vinci had to do

00:07:16.588 --> 00:07:19.007
or a scientist in the 19th or 20th century

00:07:19.007 --> 00:07:21.474
is absolutely astounding.

00:07:21.474 --> 00:07:24.286
So what you have at your
fingertips the knowledge,

00:07:24.286 --> 00:07:26.916
the things that you can
connect and learn about

00:07:26.916 --> 00:07:29.857
the potential for learning
skills is out of this world.

00:07:29.857 --> 00:07:34.154
So if you're able to overcome
the distracting elements

00:07:34.154 --> 00:07:34.987
that are.

00:07:35.945 --> 00:07:37.560
My girlfriend and I compare it to

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.680
if you ever watch a basketball game

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:43.494
and you see the opposing
team shooting free throw.

00:07:43.494 --> 00:07:46.317
And everyone behind him and the stands

00:07:46.317 --> 00:07:48.399
are all going like this with those big

00:07:48.399 --> 00:07:49.755
you know, trying to distract him,

00:07:49.755 --> 00:07:51.190
there's like hundreds of people.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:53.135
That's what the world is like now.

00:07:53.135 --> 00:07:56.065
You've got thousands of
people waving those things,

00:07:56.065 --> 00:07:58.062
you've got so many distractions.

00:07:58.062 --> 00:07:59.969
If you're able to overcome that

00:07:59.969 --> 00:08:01.662
and be a person that can focus

00:08:01.662 --> 00:08:05.962
you have this like wealth
of information and knowledge

00:08:05.962 --> 00:08:07.877
and ability to acquire skills

00:08:07.877 --> 00:08:09.386
that nobody a hundred years ago,

00:08:09.386 --> 00:08:11.391
20 years ago could dream about.

00:08:11.391 --> 00:08:15.141
Use active imagination
is deceptively simple.

00:08:18.466 --> 00:08:21.983
Often what separates creative from

00:08:21.983 --> 00:08:23.797
conventional thinkers

00:08:23.797 --> 00:08:26.547
is that they simply search wider.

00:08:27.794 --> 00:08:31.399
They use their minds to
generate possibilities

00:08:31.399 --> 00:08:33.496
that others don't think of.

00:08:33.496 --> 00:08:38.265
Then they work to verify
what they came up with.

00:08:38.265 --> 00:08:42.098
We saw a classic example
of this with Pasteur.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:46.382
Another great example is with Henry Ford.

00:08:46.382 --> 00:08:49.647
Early on in his career
in the earliest years

00:08:49.647 --> 00:08:53.814
of what would later become
the Ford Motor Company,

00:08:54.864 --> 00:08:57.697
Ford was anxious to into speed up

00:08:58.804 --> 00:09:01.177
the production of cars.

00:09:01.177 --> 00:09:04.040
It was he saw the
automobile as the ultimate

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:05.455
consumer product.

00:09:05.455 --> 00:09:07.319
But in order for it to become that,

00:09:07.319 --> 00:09:11.902
he had to greatly increase
production rate in his factory.

00:09:11.902 --> 00:09:14.933
So he spent a couple days
looking at his employees

00:09:14.933 --> 00:09:17.161
on the factory floor and trying to imagine

00:09:17.161 --> 00:09:21.976
how he could get them to
move faster from car to car.

00:09:21.976 --> 00:09:25.574
Suddenly one day he imagined
something completely different.

00:09:25.574 --> 00:09:28.388
He imagined the men standing still

00:09:28.388 --> 00:09:30.721
and the cars coming to them.

00:09:31.582 --> 00:09:32.936
He didn't know what that really meant

00:09:32.936 --> 00:09:36.083
but he decided to try it
out and lo and behold,

00:09:36.083 --> 00:09:37.667
it increased productivity

00:09:37.667 --> 00:09:40.201
by such an incredible exponential rate

00:09:40.201 --> 00:09:42.641
that it could now become
the consumer product

00:09:42.641 --> 00:09:44.224
that he envisioned.

00:09:45.535 --> 00:09:48.693
Things like antibodies or assembly lines,

00:09:48.693 --> 00:09:51.437
they seemed so obvious to us now

00:09:51.437 --> 00:09:53.787
but they are never obvious in the present.

00:09:53.787 --> 00:09:56.305
And they involve an incredible leap

00:09:56.305 --> 00:09:59.138
of what I call active imagination.

00:10:00.165 --> 00:10:04.332
The difference between
regular and active imagination

00:10:05.345 --> 00:10:09.262
is that the latter is
used consciously to reach

00:10:10.153 --> 00:10:12.248
very very practical results.

00:10:12.248 --> 00:10:14.766
It's not imagination for
coming up with wild things

00:10:14.766 --> 00:10:19.279
it's imagination used for
very practical results.

00:10:19.279 --> 00:10:21.634
Think of the active
imagination as a kind of

00:10:21.634 --> 00:10:25.441
creative muscle that you're developing.

00:10:25.441 --> 00:10:27.501
First, within the loose constraints

00:10:27.501 --> 00:10:29.174
of what you're working on

00:10:29.174 --> 00:10:33.539
you open your mind up to you
give a complete free rein.

00:10:33.539 --> 00:10:37.354
You entertain almost every
possibility that you could

00:10:37.354 --> 00:10:39.104
imagine or visualize.

00:10:40.191 --> 00:10:42.584
You engage in what Charles Darwin

00:10:42.584 --> 00:10:46.466
calls fools experiment, fools experiments.

00:10:46.466 --> 00:10:48.452
This is the fun play

00:10:48.452 --> 00:10:50.202
part of serious play.

00:10:51.102 --> 00:10:53.324
You use notebooks, sketches,

00:10:53.324 --> 00:10:56.468
diagrams, models, prototypes

00:10:56.468 --> 00:11:00.721
to help externalize the
products of your imagination.

00:11:00.721 --> 00:11:03.917
Once you have gone through this

00:11:03.917 --> 00:11:07.689
you now enter the second serious part

00:11:07.689 --> 00:11:08.939
of the process.

00:11:10.042 --> 00:11:12.885
First you choose one or few of the more

00:11:12.885 --> 00:11:14.802
promising possibilities that were

00:11:14.802 --> 00:11:16.790
generated by your imagination.

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:20.957
And now you actively work to
test verify or confirm it.

00:11:22.122 --> 00:11:25.323
This might entail launching a

00:11:25.323 --> 00:11:27.593
beta version on the public.

00:11:27.593 --> 00:11:30.679
Once you have feedback
you now go back into

00:11:30.679 --> 00:11:33.391
the first part of the
process in a shortened form

00:11:33.391 --> 00:11:35.433
using your imagination to perhaps take

00:11:35.433 --> 00:11:37.657
what you have into an even better

00:11:37.657 --> 00:11:40.550
and higher form circling
constantly through

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:42.832
this process in shorter and shorter cycles

00:11:42.832 --> 00:11:45.294
until you've got something that's both

00:11:45.294 --> 00:11:48.472
incredibly imaginative
and incredibly realistic

00:11:48.472 --> 00:11:49.639
and practical.

00:11:50.679 --> 00:11:53.591
By developing this by going
through this over and over

00:11:53.591 --> 00:11:55.546
and over again you will

00:11:55.546 --> 00:11:58.108
develop your imagination into an

00:11:58.108 --> 00:12:01.105
incredibly powerful creative muscle.

00:12:01.105 --> 00:12:05.370
What really impressed
me with someone like 50

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:09.203
is he grows up in a world
without any parents.

00:12:10.046 --> 00:12:13.342
I mean it's a hard
thing to imagine in life

00:12:13.342 --> 00:12:15.359
but try for a moment.

00:12:15.359 --> 00:12:18.029
Put yourself in those shoes

00:12:18.029 --> 00:12:19.938
he never knew his father to this day

00:12:19.938 --> 00:12:21.727
he doesn't know whose father is.

00:12:21.727 --> 00:12:25.897
His mother was murdered
when he was eight-years-old.

00:12:25.897 --> 00:12:27.397
She was a hustler.

00:12:28.386 --> 00:12:30.268
His grandparents take him in

00:12:30.268 --> 00:12:31.931
but they have nine other kids

00:12:31.931 --> 00:12:34.035
that they're looking after
from various different

00:12:34.035 --> 00:12:35.653
extended families.

00:12:35.653 --> 00:12:39.821
So he's basically has
no parental supervision

00:12:39.821 --> 00:12:41.949
from the age of eight and even before then

00:12:41.949 --> 00:12:43.587
he had very little.

00:12:43.587 --> 00:12:46.420
And so he has to find and discover

00:12:47.273 --> 00:12:49.248
everything for himself.

00:12:49.248 --> 00:12:52.754
He can't depend on other
people to give him anything.

00:12:52.754 --> 00:12:54.083
They won't give him money,

00:12:54.083 --> 00:12:55.187
they won't give him clothing

00:12:55.187 --> 00:12:56.568
they won't give him education .

00:12:56.568 --> 00:12:58.707
And so he on his own as if

00:12:58.707 --> 00:13:00.808
that metaphor being on a desert island

00:13:00.808 --> 00:13:03.749
is his life, he's on a desert island.

00:13:03.749 --> 00:13:06.616
He has to develop everything for himself.

00:13:06.616 --> 00:13:09.603
And he does, and that is what has made him

00:13:09.603 --> 00:13:12.741
a person of incredible power.

00:13:12.741 --> 00:13:14.444
Now he doesn't feel dependent on anybody

00:13:14.444 --> 00:13:17.411
or anything he knows that if anything goes

00:13:17.411 --> 00:13:19.700
wrong in life he has the resources

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:22.723
within himself to find the right answer.

00:13:22.723 --> 00:13:25.169
And so I think this is an idea that

00:13:25.169 --> 00:13:27.252
applies to an individual.

00:13:28.802 --> 00:13:31.218
It applies to a company,
to a leader, to a CEO.

00:13:31.218 --> 00:13:34.834
It applies to relationships,
self-reliance is,

00:13:34.834 --> 00:13:36.722
to me, the most, if
you're going to develop

00:13:36.722 --> 00:13:39.148
anything in life, that that has to be it.

00:13:39.148 --> 00:13:41.074
That's the most important skill.

00:13:41.074 --> 00:13:42.864
There's a phrase in
the Bible that's become

00:13:42.864 --> 00:13:47.375
kind of an expression
called suffer fools gladly.

00:13:47.375 --> 00:13:49.415
And somehow in our modern world

00:13:49.415 --> 00:13:51.619
to cut totally inverted

00:13:51.619 --> 00:13:53.973
which is something that
it kind of bothers me.

00:13:53.973 --> 00:13:57.339
The original expression meant that

00:13:57.339 --> 00:14:00.494
you're constantly surrounded by fools.

00:14:00.494 --> 00:14:02.114
Half the world are fools

00:14:02.114 --> 00:14:03.974
or whatever the percentage is.

00:14:03.974 --> 00:14:06.388
And and maybe you're a bit of a fool

00:14:06.388 --> 00:14:08.326
and what you need to do is

00:14:08.326 --> 00:14:09.384
you need to suck.

00:14:09.384 --> 00:14:12.144
You need to be able to put up with fools

00:14:12.144 --> 00:14:13.749
and laugh at them and
not take them seriously.

00:14:13.749 --> 00:14:16.485
That's what it means
in the Bible and that's

00:14:16.485 --> 00:14:17.938
the wisdom in the Bible.

00:14:17.938 --> 00:14:20.125
And then somehow it got
turned around in modern times

00:14:20.125 --> 00:14:22.677
where where it seems to be a virtue that

00:14:22.677 --> 00:14:24.781
you don't suffer fools gladly.

00:14:24.781 --> 00:14:27.084
That you don't put up with them.

00:14:27.084 --> 00:14:28.566
That you get angry about them,

00:14:28.566 --> 00:14:30.361
that you confront them,
and you try to change them

00:14:30.361 --> 00:14:32.410
and on and on and on and on and on.

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:35.788
And the idea that I try
to say it's part of my

00:14:35.788 --> 00:14:38.005
chapter on social intelligence which is

00:14:38.005 --> 00:14:40.538
I try and tell you that you're not going

00:14:40.538 --> 00:14:42.221
to be a master in this world unless you

00:14:42.221 --> 00:14:44.202
know how to get along with people.

00:14:44.202 --> 00:14:45.418
We're social animals.

00:14:45.418 --> 00:14:47.255
You can't just be a nerd,

00:14:47.255 --> 00:14:50.261
a tech guy that has no contact with people

00:14:50.261 --> 00:14:51.787
you have to learn how to be with people

00:14:51.787 --> 00:14:53.612
and be socially intelligent.

00:14:53.612 --> 00:14:56.153
And it's actually a beautiful
form of intelligence.

00:14:56.153 --> 00:14:57.697
And one of the aspects is

00:14:57.697 --> 00:14:59.788
to learn how to suffer fools gladly.

00:14:59.788 --> 00:15:02.231
And I tell you what I think a fool is.

00:15:02.231 --> 00:15:05.153
A fool is somebody that
doesn't have the right

00:15:05.153 --> 00:15:07.938
kind of sense of priorities in life.

00:15:07.938 --> 00:15:10.183
They magnify the little
things that we should

00:15:10.183 --> 00:15:14.191
just not worry about into
some huge dramatic thing.

00:15:14.191 --> 00:15:17.064
And the huge dramatic things
that completely ignore.

00:15:17.064 --> 00:15:20.337
They have no sense of
proportion and we encounter

00:15:20.337 --> 00:15:22.142
them every day in our lives.

00:15:22.142 --> 00:15:23.495
And in fact, we encounter a little bit

00:15:23.495 --> 00:15:24.328
of them in ourselves.

00:15:24.328 --> 00:15:26.327
We all have a foolish side.

00:15:26.327 --> 00:15:28.842
So let's all be patient with fools.

00:15:28.842 --> 00:15:32.468
If you try and change them or fight them

00:15:32.468 --> 00:15:35.079
you're only going to waste valuable time

00:15:35.079 --> 00:15:38.392
and energy in your short life.

00:15:38.392 --> 00:15:40.018
Learn to laugh at them, learn to know it's

00:15:40.018 --> 00:15:41.828
a part of the human comedy.

00:15:41.828 --> 00:15:43.618
I find that that's going to

00:15:43.618 --> 00:15:45.448
save you energy in the end.

00:15:45.448 --> 00:15:47.527
And I have stories in there
of how not only do you

00:15:47.527 --> 00:15:51.753
suffer fools gladly, but you
turn it around to an advantage.

00:15:51.753 --> 00:15:54.042
So I have a story in there

00:15:54.042 --> 00:15:56.856
Daniel Everett, who's a
great kind of anthropologist

00:15:56.856 --> 00:16:00.013
and linguist, and he came
out with a radical new

00:16:00.013 --> 00:16:01.708
theory on linguistics

00:16:01.708 --> 00:16:04.753
and suddenly he finds himself
being attacked by everyone

00:16:04.753 --> 00:16:06.108
in the field.

00:16:06.108 --> 00:16:07.890
But they're attacking him

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:09.468
in this ad hominine manner

00:16:09.468 --> 00:16:11.006
as if he's a charlatan.

00:16:11.006 --> 00:16:13.466
And they're not attacking his ideas

00:16:13.466 --> 00:16:14.603
and it's driving him crazy

00:16:14.603 --> 00:16:16.288
and now he's dealing with fools.

00:16:16.288 --> 00:16:18.324
And he decides that what he's going to do

00:16:18.324 --> 00:16:20.293
is he's going to turn it to his advantage.

00:16:20.293 --> 00:16:22.573
He's going to make sure it out from now on

00:16:22.573 --> 00:16:24.490
that anything he presents in a book or a

00:16:24.490 --> 00:16:26.560
lecture is airtight.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:30.044
They would usually pinpoint
one little small thing

00:16:30.044 --> 00:16:32.614
in something he said that
could be inconsistent

00:16:32.614 --> 00:16:35.193
and say therefore your
whole theory is wrong.

00:16:35.193 --> 00:16:38.509
Okay from now on I'm not going
to be anything inconsistent.

00:16:38.509 --> 00:16:41.232
So he took the foolish
aspect of these attacks

00:16:41.232 --> 00:16:43.423
and he decided he would make
it would make him stronger

00:16:43.423 --> 00:16:45.220
and would make his ideas stronger.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.499
That's what suffer fools gladly is about

00:16:47.499 --> 00:16:49.658
and I have other examples
there in the book.

00:16:49.658 --> 00:16:53.469
You learn to embrace
uncertainty and chaos.

00:16:53.469 --> 00:16:55.680
Life is inherently chaotic and it

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:58.052
doesn't fit into our tidy formulas.

00:16:58.052 --> 00:17:00.568
Not only does this not make you anxious

00:17:00.568 --> 00:17:04.915
but you find chaos and
uncertainty deeply exciting

00:17:04.915 --> 00:17:06.248
and stimulating.

00:17:07.187 --> 00:17:11.677
Slowly adopting this as
an overall philosophy

00:17:11.677 --> 00:17:13.860
will naturally loosen the mind up

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:16.381
and give it greater creative flow.

00:17:16.381 --> 00:17:20.600
You will naturally consider
more options and possibilities.

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:24.661
The way to transform yourself
is through your work.

00:17:24.661 --> 00:17:27.297
Now I know this runs counter to our

00:17:27.297 --> 00:17:30.470
prevailing cultural prejudices.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:33.803
Work is too ugly, too boring, too banal.

00:17:35.208 --> 00:17:37.487
Self-transformation,
we think, comes through

00:17:37.487 --> 00:17:39.191
a spiritual journey.

00:17:39.191 --> 00:17:42.666
Therapy, a guru who tells us what to do.

00:17:42.666 --> 00:17:46.833
Intense group experiences and
social experiences and drugs.

00:17:50.532 --> 00:17:54.276
But most of these are ways of
running away from ourselves

00:17:54.276 --> 00:17:57.229
and relieving our chronic boredom.

00:17:57.229 --> 00:17:59.685
They are not connected
to process and so any

00:17:59.685 --> 00:18:02.686
changes that occur don't last.

00:18:02.686 --> 00:18:06.820
Instead through our work
we can actually connect

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:10.396
to who we are instead of running away.

00:18:10.396 --> 00:18:13.813
And by entering that slow organic process

00:18:15.029 --> 00:18:16.862
we can actually change ourselves

00:18:16.862 --> 00:18:19.258
from the inside out in
a way that's very real

00:18:19.258 --> 00:18:20.675
and very lasting.

00:18:21.585 --> 00:18:25.117
This process involves a
journey of self-discovery

00:18:25.117 --> 00:18:29.200
that can be seen as quite
spiritual, if you like.

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:33.068
And in the end of this process

00:18:33.068 --> 00:18:34.801
we contribute something unique

00:18:34.801 --> 00:18:38.418
and meaningful to our
culture through our work

00:18:38.418 --> 00:18:41.585
which is hardly ugly, boring or banal.

00:18:42.865 --> 00:18:45.485
I want you to get over this notion

00:18:45.485 --> 00:18:49.873
that I find so annoying,
that so many people have

00:18:49.873 --> 00:18:53.878
that success and power
in life is dependent on

00:18:53.878 --> 00:18:56.287
something like genetics.

00:18:56.287 --> 00:18:59.957
Like some people are
born with a larger brain

00:18:59.957 --> 00:19:03.380
or they have wealthy parents who are able

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:06.791
to send them to the right school.

00:19:06.791 --> 00:19:10.351
Or or it's a lot a matter of luck.

00:19:10.351 --> 00:19:13.328
What really makes people
successful and powerful

00:19:13.328 --> 00:19:15.938
in life, and it's not just me saying this.

00:19:15.938 --> 00:19:18.323
I read hundreds of books on the subject.

00:19:18.323 --> 00:19:22.560
What makes people successful
is their degree of motivation.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:25.385
Okay I could repeat it a
hundred times but it's true

00:19:25.385 --> 00:19:27.162
every time I say it.

00:19:27.162 --> 00:19:29.994
When you are motivated,
when you feel yourself

00:19:29.994 --> 00:19:34.438
emotionally engaged in the
subject, you learn faster.

00:19:34.438 --> 00:19:38.074
You learn what could take
somebody 10 years to learn

00:19:38.074 --> 00:19:41.010
you can learn in two years.

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:44.741
When you feel emotionally
engaged with something

00:19:44.741 --> 00:19:48.443
you're able to push past
off all the obstacles.

00:19:48.443 --> 00:19:52.076
The sense that it's genetics
or the size of our brain

00:19:52.076 --> 00:19:56.193
or our parents money, you
can't control any of that,

00:19:56.193 --> 00:19:57.026
obviously.

00:19:57.026 --> 00:19:59.588
And they could become kind
of crutches for some people.

00:19:59.588 --> 00:20:02.015
But the amount of motivation
you feel, the emotional

00:20:02.015 --> 00:20:04.584
connection you have to what
you're studying or doing,

00:20:04.584 --> 00:20:06.372
that is something within your control.

00:20:06.372 --> 00:20:09.159
That is something you can choose to take.

00:20:09.159 --> 00:20:12.002
And it is you're going to
find people giving you all

00:20:12.002 --> 00:20:14.527
kinds of great advice about your careers

00:20:14.527 --> 00:20:17.283
about where you should go, you know,

00:20:17.283 --> 00:20:19.019
for your MBA et cetera.

00:20:19.019 --> 00:20:20.570
But if there's one piece
of advice that I think

00:20:20.570 --> 00:20:21.746
is more important than that is that

00:20:21.746 --> 00:20:24.681
is it is this idea of following these

00:20:24.681 --> 00:20:28.075
natural inclinations and
creating your own career path

00:20:28.075 --> 00:20:31.477
and finding a way to engage
the deepest motivating

00:20:31.477 --> 00:20:33.222
parts of your psyche.

00:20:33.222 --> 00:20:34.564
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:20:34.564 --> 00:20:37.464
I made this video because
Owen Forbes asked me to.

00:20:37.464 --> 00:20:39.079
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you

00:20:39.079 --> 00:20:41.762
want me to profile next
leave in the comments below

00:20:41.762 --> 00:20:42.861
I'll see what I can do.

00:20:42.861 --> 00:20:44.891
I'd also love to know
which of Robert Greene's

00:20:44.891 --> 00:20:47.621
top ten rules have the
biggest impact on you.

00:20:47.621 --> 00:20:50.098
Leave it in the comments,
I'll join in the discussion.

00:20:50.098 --> 00:20:52.617
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe

00:20:52.617 --> 00:20:54.534
and we'll see you soon.

